1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
Okay, welcome back, and the next section that we're going to cover is chronic inflammation.

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:10,200
This is just in the sequence of inflammation that we've been covering within Chapter 2.

3
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:16,440
This particular section reminds us that it starts by discussing the fact that

4
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:20,300
chronic inflammation is characterized by lymphocytes and plasma cells in tissue.

5
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:24,200
Remember that I told you that basically the idea behind inflammation is that

6
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:29,960
inflammation is getting what's present within the blood vessel into the tissue space,

7
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:33,680
and that within the blood vessel can be lymphocytes and are neutrophils.

8
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:40,520
And when you bring neutrophils out into the tissue space, that's called acute inflammation.

9
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:44,960
And when you call lymphocytes into the tissue space, that's called chronic inflammation.

10
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:47,840
And those lymphocytes can also become plasma cells.

11
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:53,700
And so the chronic inflammatory state is basically characterized by the presence of

12
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:55,820
lymphocytes and plasma cells in tissue.

13
00:00:56,180 --> 00:01:00,120
Now, it's important to remember that this is a delayed response.

14
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:01,680
It doesn't occur immediately.

15
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:04,640
It takes some time to generate, usually a couple days.

16
00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:11,980
But it's more specific than the innate immune system, more specific than the neutrophils.

17
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:15,760
And it is an example of adaptive immunity.

18
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:20,680
And we've already talked about adaptive immunity at the very beginning of this chapter.

19
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:22,680
And so I won't dwell on that here.

20
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:29,720
Here's an image just reminding you of the characteristic cells that are present in chronic inflammation.

21
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:32,660
And so you can see that all of these cells are mononuclear.

22
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:38,000
They don't have that multilobated nuclear look of a neutrophil.

23
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,480
And we can see particularly that, for example, within here is a lymphocyte, and here is a plasma cell.

24
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:55,300
Plasma cells are characterized by nuclei that are off to the edge of the cell with a little bit of this sort of glossy clearing next to the nucleus.

25
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:57,800
And you can kind of make that out over here.

26
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:59,720
I'm going to erase it so you can now see it.

27
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:11,280
But this is the plasma cell with a nucleus pushed off to the side and this little area of sort of glassy, shiny cytoplasm, which is characteristic of the plasma cell.

28
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:14,120
And you can see adjacent to it here is a lymphocyte.

29
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:18,260
And so these are the hallmark cells of chronic inflammation.

30
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:26,960
Now, chronic inflammation is going to arise in the context of certain stimuli, the same way that acute inflammation arose in the context of certain stimuli.

31
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:31,120
And the most common stimulus for chronic inflammation is persistent infection.

32
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:32,920
This really is the most common stimulus.

33
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:40,960
Other things that can lead to chronic inflammation include viruses, microbacteria, parasites, and fungi.

34
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:44,560
These all require chronic inflammation in order to be resolved.

35
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:47,500
Another example would be autoimmune disease.

36
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:52,500
So patients with autoimmune disease get abnormal, chronic inflammation.

37
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:55,900
Foreign material can result in chronic inflammation.

38
00:02:56,560 --> 00:02:58,240
And some cancers.

39
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:07,180
One of the major mechanisms by which cancers are attacked and destroyed is via a chronic inflammatory state.

40
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:12,640
And we'll talk more about cancers and inflammation when we discuss your chapter on neoplasia.

41
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:18,520
Remember that when you think about lymphocytes, lymphocytes can be divided into T cells and B cells.

42
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:27,720
And so I want to just give you a little bit of an immunology review here so that our discussions will be made so that it will be easier to discuss some of the diseases that I want to talk about.

43
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:29,000
So let's start with T cells.

44
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:30,380
T cells are made in the bone marrow.

45
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:32,660
They're born as progenitor T cells.

46
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:36,580
And after they're born as progenitor T cells, they go to the thymus to receive education.

47
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:39,620
Remember that it's in the thymus that they basically go to college.

48
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:43,320
And in the thymus, they learn how to undergo T cell rearrangement.

49
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:47,960
So their T cell receptor rearrangement, their TCR or T cell receptor undergoes rearrangement.

50
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:55,220
And those progenitor cells become CD4 positive helper cells or they can graduate and become CD8 positive cytotoxic T cells.

51
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:58,600
And all of this development normally occurs in the thymus.

52
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:04,400
Now it's important to remember that the T cell receptor complex is really the way by which T cells can see antigen.

53
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:08,120
That's their, that's the specific way by which they detect antigen.

54
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:15,360
And we say T cell receptor complex because the T cell receptor complex actually contains both the T cell receptor along with CD3.

55
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:17,760
So CD3 is also part of that complex.

56
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:20,600
And it's this complex that recognizes antigen.

57
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:25,020
Remember that it's restricted to that antigen that's presented on MHC.

58
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:29,620
T cell receptors are only able to see antigen for the most part.

59
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:36,260
Again, general principle, T cell receptors are only able to see antigen that is presented on MHC.

60
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:46,180
In particular, you need to be aware of, and I'm sure, I'm hopeful at this point, you are aware of, that CD4 positive T cells, they recognize MHC class 2.

61
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:51,520
CD8 positive T cells, they recognize antigen presented on MHC class 1.

62
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:58,160
And there's a famous mnemonic where, you know, these are CD4, and this is MHC class 2, so 2 times 4 is 8.

63
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:03,680
And these are CD8s, and this is MHC class 1, and so 8 times 1 is also 8.

64
00:05:03,960 --> 00:05:06,860
And so you always want it for, you always want to get to the number 8.

65
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:12,640
Regardless, you do need to know that CD4 positive T cells recognize MHC class 2,

66
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:19,320
and CD8 positive T cells, they recognize MHC class 1, or antigens bound to MHC class 1.

67
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:28,360
Now, activation of T cells actually requires binding of antigen and the MHC complex.

68
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:33,940
So, again, I told you that T cells will only recognize antigen that is bound to the MHC complex.

69
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:42,060
However, the T cell, once it recognizes the antigen bound to the MHC complex, that's not sufficient enough for the T cell to be activated.

70
00:05:42,060 --> 00:05:51,380
It is going to require an additional second signal before the T cell will actually be allowed to become activated and then produce its function.

71
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:56,480
And so let's talk about how T cells get activated, because that is important for examinations.

72
00:05:56,900 --> 00:06:01,800
CD4 positive T cell activation occurs via extracellular antigen.

73
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:02,940
Now, let's go back.

74
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:07,120
You know that CD4 positive T cells, they recognize MHC class 2.

75
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:11,220
And MHC class 2 is predominantly present on antigen-presenting cells.

76
00:06:11,220 --> 00:06:13,180
What do antigen-presenting cells do?

77
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:21,840
They basically take antigen from the outside world, they phagocytose it, they process it, and then they express it on MHC class 2.

78
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:25,040
So an example would be macrophages or dendritic cells.

79
00:06:25,340 --> 00:06:32,880
These are cells that have the ability to phagocytose extracellular antigen, present it on MHC class 2, and show it to the CD4 positive T cell.

80
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:37,680
Now, the second signal, remember that the CD4 positive T cell requires a second signal.

81
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:41,040
Basically, all T cell activation requires a second signal.

82
00:06:41,420 --> 00:06:51,500
And so the second signal is going to be B7, which is present on the antigen-presenting cell, will bind CD28 on the CD4 positive T cell.

83
00:06:52,020 --> 00:06:54,100
And that provides a second activation signal.

84
00:06:54,240 --> 00:07:00,100
And if you're having trouble remembering that, then you can remember that 28 divided by 7 is 4.

85
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:02,440
So that provides a second activation signal.

86
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:09,160
Now, once CD84 positive T cells are activated, then they will secrete cytokines.

87
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:11,240
And these cytokines help inflammation.

88
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:15,480
And that's why they're called CD4 positive helper T cells, because they help inflammation.

89
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:22,100
And interestingly, the CD4 positive T cells will be divided into two subsets.

90
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:28,240
And the two subsets will actually help the two types of inflammation that occur in chronic inflammation.

91
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:31,220
Why don't I take you one slide back and just go over this briefly.

92
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:37,320
So the CD4 positive T cell, right, the CD4 positive T cell's job is to help.

93
00:07:37,540 --> 00:07:39,820
And it's going to help two types of inflammation.

94
00:07:40,380 --> 00:07:49,300
It can help B cells, right, or it can help CD8 positive T cells.

95
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:53,140
And these are the two types of inflammatory responses.

96
00:07:53,780 --> 00:07:57,220
And these are the two things that helper T cells will help.

97
00:07:57,700 --> 00:08:01,340
And so they're actually helper T cells are divided into two subsets.

98
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:03,400
The first is the Th1 subset.

99
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:07,920
And the Th1 subset helps the CD8 positive T cells.

100
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:13,760
Now, it helps the CD8 positive T cells by generating IL-2, which is a T cell growth factor,

101
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:15,760
and a CD8 positive T cell activator.

102
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:22,880
And it also helps the CD8 positive T cells by activating macrophages via interferon gamma.

103
00:08:23,820 --> 00:08:27,740
So these are the key products of the Th1 subset.

104
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:33,820
And you do have to be familiar with these products for examinations.

105
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:37,080
And you do need to know what they actually do as well.

106
00:08:37,900 --> 00:08:42,640
The Th2 subset, what it does, it helps the B cells.

107
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:47,060
And what it helps the B cells by producing IL-4 and IL-5.

108
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:50,280
These are really the two big molecules that you need to be familiar with.

109
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:58,100
The IL-4 causes class switching to IgG and IgE.

110
00:08:58,580 --> 00:09:04,480
And the IL-5 helps to bring in eosinophils, causes maturation of B cells to plasma cells,

111
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:07,140
and allows for class switching to IgA.

112
00:09:07,140 --> 00:09:12,020
Now, another product of the Th2 subset is to produce IL-10.

113
00:09:13,140 --> 00:09:15,620
And IL-10 inhibits the Th1 phenotype.

114
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:20,580
We've already also talked about IL-10 in the context of, do you recall where we talked about IL-10?

115
00:09:21,460 --> 00:09:25,480
When I talked to you about macrophages and how the macrophages come in

116
00:09:25,480 --> 00:09:27,460
and they manage the last step of acute inflammation,

117
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:34,480
and I said that one possibility was resolution of the inflammatory response.

118
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:40,740
And so, remember that the macrophages produced IL-10 there to sort of shut down the inflammatory response.

119
00:09:41,260 --> 00:09:47,500
So, anyway, this is the key function of the Th2 subset of helper T cells.

120
00:09:48,540 --> 00:09:52,460
Now, that's the way by which CD4 positive T cells are activated.

121
00:09:52,840 --> 00:09:56,100
CD8 positive T cells are going to be activated by the following.

122
00:09:56,100 --> 00:10:00,680
Well, first of all, CD8 positive T cells, they recognize MHC class 1.

123
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:06,380
And we need to go back and review from basic immunology, how does antigen get loaded on MHC class 1?

124
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:08,800
Well, it's intracellular antigen.

125
00:10:09,160 --> 00:10:17,000
Remember that basically a sampling of the protein being produced within the cell gets loaded on MHC class 1

126
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:24,940
and allows the CD8 positive T cells to determine whether there is a foreign protein being produced within the cell.

127
00:10:24,940 --> 00:10:30,300
And so, MHC class 1 predominantly expresses intracellular antigen.

128
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:36,780
The CD8 positive T cell recognizes MHC class 1, and that really is the first signal.

129
00:10:37,120 --> 00:10:39,840
But recall that T cells require an additional signal.

130
00:10:40,260 --> 00:10:45,000
And so, the second signal is IL-2 from the CD4 positive T cell.

131
00:10:45,060 --> 00:10:49,500
Remember that the CD4 positive helper 1 subtype, the Th1 subtype,

132
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:52,580
it helps CD8 positive T cells, as I've already explained.

133
00:10:52,980 --> 00:10:56,960
And the IL-2 from the helper T cell allows for the second signal,

134
00:10:57,280 --> 00:11:00,080
allowing the CD8 positive T cells to become activated.

135
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:03,640
Once the CD8 positive T cells are activated for killing,

136
00:11:04,940 --> 00:11:07,240
the way by which they kill the cells is that they actually,

137
00:11:07,420 --> 00:11:12,940
the way by which they kill the antigen is to actually kill the cell in which the antigen is present.

138
00:11:12,940 --> 00:11:16,640
And we've already talked about that when we talked about apoptosis.

139
00:11:17,100 --> 00:11:19,300
So, how do CD8 positive T cells kill their target?

140
00:11:19,740 --> 00:11:25,040
They secrete perforins, and that perforates a hole in the cytoplasm of the target.

141
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:30,520
Granzyme enters, and once granzyme enters, it induces apoptosis of the target cell.

142
00:11:30,900 --> 00:11:33,320
That's the way by which CD8 positive T cells kill.

143
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:37,080
Now, do you remember, I'm going to try to jog your memory here

144
00:11:37,080 --> 00:11:39,920
and remind you of a high yield that shows up on examinations.

145
00:11:40,420 --> 00:11:44,960
What is the key enzyme that mediates apoptosis that the,

146
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:47,340
in this particular case, that granzyme would activate?

147
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:48,880
Caspase.

148
00:11:49,220 --> 00:11:52,400
So, remember that caspases are the key mediators of apoptosis.

149
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:53,460
That's high yield, okay?

150
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,080
Now, that's one way by which the CD8 positive T cells will kill.

151
00:11:57,260 --> 00:11:59,800
Another way by which the CD8 positive T cells kill

152
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:02,420
is that they actually express something called Fas ligand.

153
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:06,920
Now, Fas ligand has the ability to bind Fas on the target cell,

154
00:12:07,080 --> 00:12:09,360
and Fas is an activator of apoptosis.

155
00:12:09,880 --> 00:12:13,780
And if you recall, again, sort of, let's do a little circular review.

156
00:12:14,320 --> 00:12:18,160
Remember that there are three ways by which we activate the caspases.

157
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:19,180
Do you remember those?

158
00:12:19,620 --> 00:12:23,600
So, one way by which we activate the caspases was the intrinsic mitochondrial pathway,

159
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:28,200
and that was really driven by cytochrome C leaking out from the mitochondria

160
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:29,520
and activating the caspases.

161
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:34,960
Another way that we activate the caspases was via extrinsic receptor pathway,

162
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:39,280
and an example of that was Fas-Fas ligand.

163
00:12:39,420 --> 00:12:43,240
Another example of that was TNF binding the cells.

164
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:45,820
And then a third way by which we activate apoptosis

165
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:49,920
is the CD8 positive T cell dumping granzyme,

166
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:53,420
which then activates the caspases, which activates apoptosis.

167
00:12:53,940 --> 00:12:58,000
So, again, this is the mechanism by which CD8 positive T cells kill their target.

168
00:12:58,800 --> 00:12:59,900
Okay, B lymphocytes.

169
00:13:00,020 --> 00:13:01,520
Let's go to B lymphocytes very briefly.

170
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:04,860
B lymphocytes are produced as immature B cells in the bone marrow,

171
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:10,280
and they undergo Ig rearrangement to become naive B cells.

172
00:13:10,740 --> 00:13:16,160
So, basically, the naive B cells, they are sort of the baby B cells,

173
00:13:16,280 --> 00:13:19,380
and they express on their surface IgM and IgD.

174
00:13:19,780 --> 00:13:24,360
So, the very initial B cell does express IgM and IgD on its surface.

175
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:27,760
Now, how do B cells get activated?

176
00:13:27,900 --> 00:13:28,640
There's two ways.

177
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:33,700
The first way by which B cells get activated is antigen binds the IgM or IgD.

178
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:35,400
So, for example, this is a B cell.

179
00:13:35,760 --> 00:13:37,240
It's got a surface receptor.

180
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:40,420
Let's say that this surface receptor is actually IgM.

181
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:41,240
All right?

182
00:13:41,440 --> 00:13:42,480
An antigen comes.

183
00:13:42,620 --> 00:13:44,680
It binds the surface IgM.

184
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:46,800
That sends a signal to the B cell,

185
00:13:47,100 --> 00:13:49,460
and this B cell will then become a plasma cell,

186
00:13:49,700 --> 00:13:53,440
and it will become an IgM-secreting plasma cell.

187
00:13:53,760 --> 00:13:56,440
So, that's one way by which B cells can become activated.

188
00:13:56,440 --> 00:14:05,640
Another way by which B cells can become activated is that the B cell can present antigen to the CD4-positive T cell by MHC class 2.

189
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,960
The CD4-positive T cell will then interact with the B cell.

190
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:12,520
The second signal, that's the first signal.

191
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:19,900
The second signal for the helper T cell will be that CD40 on the B cell will bind CD40 ligand on the helper T cell,

192
00:14:19,900 --> 00:14:22,760
and that then activates the B cell.

193
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:23,420
All right?

194
00:14:23,740 --> 00:14:28,840
So, this is actually very high yield, and you'll see why when we come into immune deficiency.

195
00:14:29,320 --> 00:14:31,760
But there are two ways by which the B cell can be activated.

196
00:14:31,860 --> 00:14:34,840
The first would be for antigen to bind surface IgM,

197
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:39,080
which then causes the B cell to become a plasma cell and secrete IgM.

198
00:14:39,080 --> 00:14:45,660
And the second would be for the B cell to consume the antigen, to present the antigen on MHC class 2,

199
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:48,680
for the CD4-positive T cell to then recognize it,

200
00:14:48,920 --> 00:14:53,520
and then the B cell would give a second signal, which is CD40, to the helper T cell,

201
00:14:53,760 --> 00:14:59,040
and that CD40 second signal would provide the activation signal that would then activate the T cell.

202
00:14:59,400 --> 00:15:02,320
And, of course, activation of the helper T cell is going to result in what?

203
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:05,160
It's going to result in production of IL-4 and IL-5,

204
00:15:05,460 --> 00:15:11,400
and that's going to allow for B cells to isotype switch, undergo hypermutation, and mature to plasma cells,

205
00:15:11,520 --> 00:15:18,220
so that these B cells will now be able to secrete IgE, IgG, IgA,

206
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:23,660
because, remember, the B cell starts as secreting IgM or IgD.

207
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:32,400
But in order to go beyond IgM or IgD to be able to secrete IgG, IgE, and IgA,

208
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:38,560
you're actually going to need to undergo this type of reaction from the helper T cell.

209
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:39,380
Is that clear?

210
00:15:39,700 --> 00:15:43,300
And, again, I'm going to highlight this a little bit later when we talk about immune deficiency.

211
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:48,740
Okay, next sort of sub-discussion, and that is granulomatous inflammation.

212
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:52,640
Now, when you talk about chronic inflammation, well, why don't we take a step back?

213
00:15:52,640 --> 00:15:56,520
When you talk about inflammation, inflammation is divided into acute and chronic.

214
00:15:57,040 --> 00:15:58,740
And then when you talk about chronic inflammation,

215
00:15:58,960 --> 00:16:02,740
chronic inflammation is subdivided into granulomatous and non-granulomatous.

216
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:06,860
So granulomatous inflammation is a subtype of chronic inflammation.

217
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:12,000
Now, the characteristic of granulomatous inflammation is a granuloma, hence the name,

218
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:15,160
and the key cell, this is very important,

219
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:19,300
the key cell that defines a granuloma is an epithelioid histiocyte.

220
00:16:19,780 --> 00:16:21,500
All right, this is a very common exam question.

221
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:26,500
They can ask, or examiners can ask, what is the key characteristic,

222
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:29,640
what is the defining characteristic of a granuloma?

223
00:16:29,960 --> 00:16:32,260
And the answer is epithelioid histiocyte.

224
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:34,120
What is an epithelioid histiocyte?

225
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:37,940
Now, macrophages, normally they have a foamy cytoplasm.

226
00:16:38,020 --> 00:16:39,920
It's a relatively clear cytoplasm.

227
00:16:39,920 --> 00:16:44,920
However, when macrophages get activated, they can actually become activated

228
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:49,680
so that they develop abundant pink cytoplasm, and those are called epithelioid histiocytes.

229
00:16:50,140 --> 00:16:54,680
And the aggregate of epithelioid histiocytes is called granuloma.

230
00:16:54,800 --> 00:16:58,300
So when you have aggregation of epithelioid histiocytes, that's called a granuloma.

231
00:16:58,780 --> 00:17:01,080
Now, there are some additional features of a granuloma.

232
00:17:01,080 --> 00:17:06,620
They're not necessary for a granuloma, but they can also, or they're usually also seen with a granuloma.

233
00:17:06,740 --> 00:17:10,220
For example, giant cells are usually also seen with a granuloma.

234
00:17:10,680 --> 00:17:13,900
And a rim of lymphocytes are also usually seen with granuloma.

235
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:15,380
But let me make it very clear.

236
00:17:15,880 --> 00:17:22,080
The defining feature of a granuloma is an epithelioid histiocyte, high yield.

237
00:17:22,080 --> 00:17:23,960
Please keep that in the back of your mind.

238
00:17:24,980 --> 00:17:31,420
All right, one last point, and that is that granulomas can be divided into non-caseating and caseating subtypes.

239
00:17:31,800 --> 00:17:33,940
So let's kind of touch on this very briefly.

240
00:17:34,340 --> 00:17:37,840
Now, the reason it's important to recognize or understand granulomatous inflammation

241
00:17:37,840 --> 00:17:42,980
is because granulomatous inflammation actually carries a very particular differential diagnosis.

242
00:17:43,240 --> 00:17:47,680
There are a set or a limited number of things that actually give you granulomatous inflammation.

243
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:54,620
And so by knowing that there's granulomatous inflammation, you get a very distinct differential

244
00:17:54,620 --> 00:17:59,540
for what is actually driving the inflammatory process in that particular patient.

245
00:18:00,060 --> 00:18:04,240
So the first type of granuloma, as I've mentioned, is a non-caseating granuloma.

246
00:18:04,620 --> 00:18:07,160
And it's defined by the fact that it lacks central necrosis.

247
00:18:07,220 --> 00:18:08,520
And I'll show you a picture in a minute.

248
00:18:08,900 --> 00:18:13,240
But basically, these types of granulomas, they arise in reaction to foreign material.

249
00:18:13,240 --> 00:18:20,680
So, for example, if a patient has a history of breast cancer, the breast is removed,

250
00:18:21,100 --> 00:18:25,320
and the patient gets implants in the breast as part of a reconstruction,

251
00:18:25,860 --> 00:18:31,980
it's possible that those implants could leak, and they would release foreign material into the lymphatics,

252
00:18:32,060 --> 00:18:36,780
which would then go into the axillary lymph node and produce enlarged lymph nodes within the axilla.

253
00:18:37,100 --> 00:18:39,980
So that would be an example of reaction to foreign material.

254
00:18:39,980 --> 00:18:44,500
And it's important because if a patient has a history of breast cancer and has implants,

255
00:18:44,940 --> 00:18:49,440
and let's say that the patient comes in and says that she feels lymph nodes within her axilla,

256
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:51,620
you know, there's a differential diagnosis there.

257
00:18:51,700 --> 00:18:57,180
The possibility is that the patient has recurrence of her breast cancer or maybe spread of her breast cancer,

258
00:18:57,240 --> 00:18:59,140
and it's now involving the axilla.

259
00:18:59,540 --> 00:19:04,920
Or it's also possible that there's a reaction to foreign material, i.e. the foreign material from the implant,

260
00:19:05,080 --> 00:19:06,560
which is then creating granuloma.

261
00:19:06,560 --> 00:19:11,960
So if a biopsy was performed and there was cancer, that would be very different than a biopsy being performed to the lymph nodes

262
00:19:11,960 --> 00:19:14,360
and showing non-caseating granulomas.

263
00:19:14,660 --> 00:19:15,560
So that's one example.

264
00:19:16,500 --> 00:19:18,220
Another example would be sarcoidosis.

265
00:19:18,820 --> 00:19:21,520
Sarcoidosis is a disease that we'll talk about when we do respiratory.

266
00:19:22,240 --> 00:19:29,820
However, its characteristic hallmark is the production of non-caseating granulomas in multiple tissues throughout the body,

267
00:19:29,920 --> 00:19:31,040
in particular the lung.

268
00:19:31,040 --> 00:19:34,060
But again, we'll touch on that when we do the respiratory lecture.

269
00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:41,260
Beryllium can also cause the formation of non-caseating granulomas predominantly within the lung,

270
00:19:41,400 --> 00:19:42,600
but in other tissues as well.

271
00:19:43,020 --> 00:19:45,660
And beryllium and sarcoidosis are sort of on the same,

272
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:50,580
sort of can create a very similar clinical picture, especially on exams.

273
00:19:50,580 --> 00:19:56,680
So I will talk about beryllium and sarcoidosis in much more detail when we do the lung section,

274
00:19:57,060 --> 00:20:01,760
but it is a classic exam type question that we'll discuss when we get there.

275
00:20:02,340 --> 00:20:06,080
Crohn's disease is classically associated with non-caseating granulomas.

276
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:09,740
If I asked you what's the histologic hallmark of Crohn's disease,

277
00:20:09,820 --> 00:20:12,280
you would tell me non-caseating granulomas.

278
00:20:12,440 --> 00:20:16,140
If I asked you what's the histologic hallmark of ulcerative colitis,

279
00:20:16,280 --> 00:20:19,180
which is sort of the sister to Crohn's disease, what would the answer be?

280
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:22,200
The answer would be crypt abscesses.

281
00:20:22,660 --> 00:20:25,400
And crypt abscesses basically mean that if you look at the colon,

282
00:20:25,560 --> 00:20:29,400
you know that the colon is made up of a bunch of crypts that sort of line up as a test tube,

283
00:20:29,700 --> 00:20:31,920
and in the bottom of these crypts you get neutrophils.

284
00:20:31,920 --> 00:20:36,280
And so those are called crypt abscesses, or crypt abscess,

285
00:20:36,580 --> 00:20:39,340
and that is the histologic hallmark of ulcerative colitis,

286
00:20:39,680 --> 00:20:43,640
whereas the histologic hallmark of Crohn's disease is a non-caseating granuloma.

287
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:45,800
And again, you have to know the histologic hallmark.

288
00:20:46,160 --> 00:20:47,820
It's very important to be aware of.

289
00:20:48,260 --> 00:20:51,480
And then finally, another example, and again, this is a limited list.

290
00:20:51,560 --> 00:20:54,620
You could keep going, but another example would be cat scratch disease.

291
00:20:54,840 --> 00:21:01,040
And cat scratch disease, the classic description of those granulomas

292
00:21:01,040 --> 00:21:05,400
are non-caseating granulomas, and what's very important is usually it's in the neck,

293
00:21:05,720 --> 00:21:08,660
and it gives you a stellate-shaped granuloma.

294
00:21:09,040 --> 00:21:13,020
And that's a very classic thing to be aware of for examinations,

295
00:21:13,120 --> 00:21:16,760
that cat scratch disease gives you a stellate-shaped granuloma.

296
00:21:17,220 --> 00:21:18,280
All right, so let's keep moving.

297
00:21:18,400 --> 00:21:21,300
This is a picture of a non-caseating granuloma.

298
00:21:21,720 --> 00:21:24,200
And what I mean by, first of all, why is it a granuloma?

299
00:21:24,200 --> 00:21:31,020
Because you've got this aggregate here of these histiocytes with pink cytoplasm,

300
00:21:31,080 --> 00:21:34,540
and these are epithelioid histiocytes, so that makes it a granuloma.

301
00:21:34,800 --> 00:21:38,140
You can see that there are a lot of lymphocytes associated with it,

302
00:21:38,780 --> 00:21:40,880
and so that's an additional bonus.

303
00:21:41,240 --> 00:21:43,340
And then you can see the production of these giant cells,

304
00:21:43,440 --> 00:21:46,560
where you have a cell with a lot of cytoplasm and multiple nuclei.

305
00:21:46,700 --> 00:21:47,640
That's a giant cell.

306
00:21:47,720 --> 00:21:50,540
It's a multinucleated cell with abundant cytoplasm.

307
00:21:50,940 --> 00:21:52,880
And so here is sort of the classic granuloma.

308
00:21:52,880 --> 00:21:55,820
Now you'll notice that when you look at this granuloma,

309
00:21:55,960 --> 00:21:58,580
each of the histiocytes has their nucleus present.

310
00:21:58,820 --> 00:22:00,260
So this is non-caseating.

311
00:22:00,480 --> 00:22:02,160
It is not necrotic, okay?

312
00:22:02,720 --> 00:22:07,640
And so that is the histologic hallmark of a non-caseating granuloma.

313
00:22:08,380 --> 00:22:12,200
Now, the opposite of that would be a caseating granuloma.

314
00:22:12,300 --> 00:22:15,720
Let me do this histology first and then walk you through the prior slide.

315
00:22:16,020 --> 00:22:18,180
But this is an example also of a granuloma.

316
00:22:19,480 --> 00:22:21,660
Here, what we're seeing is the edge here.

317
00:22:21,660 --> 00:22:25,440
Actually, you can more appropriately make out the epithelioid histiocytes.

318
00:22:25,440 --> 00:22:31,040
But what I want to highlight within this aggregate here is that in the center, this is all dead.

319
00:22:31,420 --> 00:22:33,100
We don't really see the nuclei.

320
00:22:33,260 --> 00:22:35,820
We see sort of this pink debris and this blue debris.

321
00:22:36,160 --> 00:22:38,060
We're not really able to make out the cells as well.

322
00:22:38,140 --> 00:22:38,880
Let's compare again.

323
00:22:38,960 --> 00:22:40,000
So this is A.

324
00:22:40,660 --> 00:22:41,600
Erase the markings.

325
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:45,060
This is A, which was a non-caseating granuloma.

326
00:22:45,440 --> 00:22:46,640
And you can see this is B.

327
00:22:46,640 --> 00:22:50,200
And in B, you can see that there is necrosis and cell death.

328
00:22:50,500 --> 00:22:53,100
And so that's what makes up the caseating granuloma.

329
00:22:53,660 --> 00:22:56,780
Now, what's the key differential diagnosis for the caseating granuloma?

330
00:22:57,140 --> 00:23:00,900
Well, the caseating granuloma is the key differential is TB and fungus.

331
00:23:01,840 --> 00:23:02,140
All right?

332
00:23:02,140 --> 00:23:08,100
So that whenever you have a caseating granuloma, sort of number one and number two on your list are going to be TB and fungus.

333
00:23:08,560 --> 00:23:15,400
And so one thing that we can do as pathologists, and you do have to be familiar with this, is we can do something called an AFB stain.

334
00:23:16,200 --> 00:23:19,080
And the AFB stain allows us to look for TB.

335
00:23:19,560 --> 00:23:24,800
And we can also do something called a silver stain, also known as a GMS stain.

336
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,060
And the GMS stain allows us to look for fungus.

337
00:23:27,060 --> 00:23:37,920
And so whenever we have a caseating granuloma, we want to make sure that the AFB stain is negative and the GMS stain is done as well to ensure that there is no TB and that there is no fungus.

338
00:23:39,140 --> 00:23:39,440
Okay.

339
00:23:40,020 --> 00:23:45,280
Now, the next important thing to be aware of are the steps that are involved in granuloma formation.

340
00:23:45,660 --> 00:23:47,340
So how are granulomas formed?

341
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:49,240
Very high yield for examinations.

342
00:23:49,460 --> 00:23:52,320
I'm going to tell you a couple things that could easily show up on an exam.

343
00:23:52,320 --> 00:24:02,680
First of all, the formation of a granuloma is an interaction between macrophages and CD4 positive Th1 helper T cells.

344
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:03,280
Okay.

345
00:24:03,300 --> 00:24:04,140
That's 0.1.

346
00:24:04,320 --> 00:24:06,140
That's the first thing I would say you have to know.

347
00:24:06,540 --> 00:24:08,820
Now, what happens is the macrophages find the antigen.

348
00:24:09,240 --> 00:24:13,040
They express it in MHC class 2 because they are antigen-presenting cells.

349
00:24:13,460 --> 00:24:15,500
And the helper T cells, they recognize that.

350
00:24:15,500 --> 00:24:23,480
When they recognize, when that interaction occurs, the macrophages then secrete IL-12.

351
00:24:23,680 --> 00:24:25,020
That's high yield, IL-12.

352
00:24:25,460 --> 00:24:30,480
IL-12 induces the CD4 positive T cells into the Th1 subtype.

353
00:24:30,960 --> 00:24:37,880
The Th1 subtype cells then secrete interferon gamma, and that's what converts the macrophages to epithelioid histiocytes.

354
00:24:38,340 --> 00:24:41,140
Now, I realize there's a lot of words on this slide, but I want to really make it simple.

355
00:24:41,140 --> 00:24:48,880
Step 1, the macrophages find an antigen presented via MHC class 2 to the helper T cells.

356
00:24:49,260 --> 00:24:52,080
The helper T cells, okay, bind the antigen.

357
00:24:52,460 --> 00:25:03,340
Once that first binding occurs, the macrophages sort of then dump IL-12 and the CD4 positive Th, which then converts the CD4 positive cells to a Th1 subtype.

358
00:25:03,340 --> 00:25:10,540
The Th1 helper cells then secrete interferon gamma, which then converts the macrophages to epithelioid histiocytes.

359
00:25:11,140 --> 00:25:19,820
So, you have to know IL-12 from the macrophages, and you have to know interferon gamma from the Th1 helper T cells.

360
00:25:20,160 --> 00:25:21,120
So, that should be clear.

361
00:25:21,520 --> 00:25:25,020
Now, I want to make this very clear because this is the way the examiners go after it.

362
00:25:25,020 --> 00:25:33,800
This step, the step of IL-12 and interferon gamma, it occurs in both caseating and non-caseating granulomas.

363
00:25:33,800 --> 00:25:40,420
So, both caseating granulomas and non-caseating granulomas are both formed in this particular way.

364
00:25:40,660 --> 00:25:47,800
That really closes off our discussion of chronic inflammation, and we're going to take a little break here and then come back and discuss primary immunodeficiency.

365
00:25:47,800 --> 00:25:48,700
Thank you.

366
00:25:48,700 --> 00:25:48,920
Thank you.

367
00:25:51,740 --> 00:26:08,800
Thank you.

